xargs on OS-9 -- what was changed================================Gordon A. Moffett's xargs, mod.sources volume 3 issue 106 (1986), areimplementation of System V xargs.  ORIG/ holds the posting.Two changes, both under #ifdef OSK.1.  The command no longer goes through system().    system() launches nothing in a program built here.  Measured with a    five-line probe: system("cat SYS/motd") returns a large number, no    child runs, and `> file' in the command line never creates the file,    while the same program forking the same command with os9exec() runs    it and prints its output.  Both a -qm build and a cio-linked build    of that probe behave the same way, so it is not the choice of C    library.  An archive binary that shells out (eo) does work, so the    difference is between programs built here and programs built by    their authors; the mechanism is not yet known.    docmd() therefore splits the command line on blanks into an argv and    forks the first word directly:        os9exec(os9fork, av[0], av, environ, 0, 0, 3)    then waits for it.  That is what system() would have done.  The    limits this brings are honest ones: no shell is involved, so a    redirection or a pipe written inside the xargs command line is not    interpreted, and the command is looked up as given.2.  The original begins the assembled line with "exec ", so that the    shell replaces itself with the command.  ksh -- the shell this C    library's system() forks -- has no such usage, and every command    silently did nothing.  With the fork above, the line is the command    itself, so the prefix is gone.Worth knowing when testing it: there is no `echo' BINARY on this disk,so `xargs echo' answers "cannot run echo" -- correctly.  Use `xargs cat'or `xargs wc'.